{"id":"h064","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"A glass contains 0.25 kg of Omni-Cola (mostly water) initially at 25°C. How much ice, initially at -20°C must you add to obtain a final temperature of 0°C with all the ice melted? Neglect the heat capacity of the glass. (Unit: g)\nIs the correct answer \"70\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"70 g is the correct answer to within rounding.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The heat released by the cola cooling from 25°C to 0°C equals the heat absorbed by the ice warming and melting.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The cola releases about 26,160 J, treating it as water with c = 4186 J/(kg·K).","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Each kilogram of ice absorbs about 376,000 J in warming from -20°C to 0°C and then melting.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The required ice mass is about 0.0696 kg, or 69.6 g, which rounds to 70 g.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The result depends on the assumed constants, such as c_ice and the cola's specific heat.","confidence":0.6,"lean":-0.2,"theta":0.9425,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Different constants change the answer only slightly, in the first or second significant figure.","confidence":0.6,"lean":0.2,"theta":0.9425,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The cola is only mostly water, so its specific heat is approximate.","confidence":0.5,"lean":-0.2,"theta":0.7854,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Neglecting the glass is stated in the problem and does not affect the result.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.3,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Latent heat of fusion and water's specific heat are the most important constants, and both are standard.","confidence":0.8,"lean":1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"